[That bassist is 100% going to make it big.]

Later, Zhou Huai would come to suspect that this message had been pure, unadulterated [[poison milk|In Chinese internet slang, “poison milk” (毒奶) means making an overly confident prediction that something will go well — only for it to backfire and jinx the person or situation.]].

Because less than three seconds after he received it, the entire livehouse plunged into darkness, bringing the performance to an abrupt halt.

The heat and exhilaration from moments earlier vanished as though swallowed by a black hole. Silence descended. Every emotion in the room seemed suspended in a vacuum.

“Fuck? Did the power go out?”

That one voice cracked the shell of silence, and a flood of confused chatter followed.

It wasn't just the electricity that had been cut off. It was the future of this new band onstage.

At least, that's how Zhou Huai saw it.

“The wristband lights are off too. Does that mean our votes don't count anymore? They'll come back when the power does, right?”

“And what if they don't? There are only three votes in the preliminary round. I still need to vote for the band I actually like!”

“If they don't come back, then you just don't get to vote again. Tough luck for them.”

For a live performance, atmosphere was everything. Once the spark was extinguished and the momentum broken, not even the gods could bring it back.

Worse still, every vote that would determine the band's fate had just been wiped out.

Talk about bad luck.

The enclosed livehouse had become a dark, airless box. Complaints piled on top of complaints, each one stepping on the heels of the last, making everyone increasingly restless.

The guy from the message — “that bassist” — was still standing onstage. Compared with his two bandmates, he looked almost like an outsider, completely unbothered.

One hand rested on the neck of his bass, while the other lay casually on the microphone stand. His fingers were even tapping out a gentle rhythm.

A few flashlight beams flickered on at the side of the stage. Probably staff checking what had gone wrong. Thin, faint shafts of light swept across the musicians. They weren't bright enough to make out anyone's face, but even their blurred silhouettes were striking.

With talent and looks like that, it would be difficult not to make it big.

Just like Qin Yiyu had back then.

What a shame. His luck was terrible. He'd run straight into something beyond anyone's control. If they couldn't even get through the preliminary round, the band was practically doomed.

Suddenly, the hand resting on the microphone stand lifted slightly. The guy moved his fingers toward the restless crowd below, almost like a wave, though the gesture was difficult to decipher.

And the person he seemed to be gesturing at...

Was apparently standing right beside Zhou Huai.

Even while listening to the performance, one thought had crossed Zhou Huai's mind: this guy was quiet and reserved, but his hands seemed to speak just as much as his eyes did.

It made Zhou Huai think back to the first time he'd met him, five days earlier.

That day, he'd gone to the post office to collect a letter. On the way back, he'd received a phone call, so he drove toward the tattoo shop while making jokes and talking nonsense.

“I'm not a debt collector, but someone's already found their way to your front door...”

When he was almost there, he spotted a gap at the entrance to the alley and squeezed his car into it. He opened the envelope and skimmed the letter.

His heart sank a little.

Without saying a word, he shoved it back inside and hid the envelope in the center console.

To keep the other person from noticing anything strange, he made his voice considerably more exaggerated than usual.

“Well, sounds like you've got yourself a stalker!”

As soon as he got out of the car, a sweet fragrance hit him.

“Whoa. That roasted sweet potato smells amazing.”

He quickly returned to the conversation, slipping into his usual cheeky tone.

“Could it be some lovesick [[girl|The original word here, 果儿, is colloquial and playful. Zhou Huai is teasing the other person about a girl who's still hung up on him.]]? After all this time, and she's still thinking about you?”

The person on the other end cursed at him. Zhou Huai laughed and looked up, spotting a little street stall.

But when he got a proper look at the person running it, he stopped in his tracks.

“Fuck.”

“Since when do guys this handsome start running street stalls?”

An elderly man with a slight limp was approaching the stall too. Zhou Huai stepped aside to let him through, then waited behind him, taking the opportunity to have a good look at the handsome young man.

The guy was strangely quiet. Even when customers approached, he didn't say a word.

He was dressed entirely in black, with a dark grey baseball cap pulled low over his forehead, easily hiding half his face. It was only an old motorcycle jacket, really, but somehow he made it look ridiculously good. Like a model: handsome, well-proportioned, slim waist, long legs.

His hair was pretty long, too.

When he lowered his head, Zhou Huai caught sight of a little bun tied at the back, along with a row of silver earrings running along his right ear. The late-summer sunlight made them glitter.

Zhou Huai had been so busy staring that he hadn't noticed the elderly man had been trying to communicate for a while.

His mouth was open, but he wasn't making a sound. He could only gesture with his hands, looking rather troubled.

The handsome young man with the cold expression watched him for a moment, then pulled a hand out of his pocket.

To Zhou Huai's surprise, he began signing fluently.

“Holy shit?”

The person on the phone still hadn't hung up. His voice came through lazily.

[What? Did your sweet potato grow legs and run off with a handsome guy?]

“Good grief.” Now that he knew the guy couldn't hear him, Zhou Huai didn't bother lowering his voice. He sighed. “And he's deaf, too? What a waste. A handsome guy like that...”

The elderly man paid and left.

Zhou Huai pointed at the roasting oven, held up one finger, then paused and made a gesture asking the person on the phone to wait.

“Hey, Qin Yiyu. Want one?”

What he didn't notice was that the handsome guy in front of him had suddenly raised his head.

“No? Fine. Don't come whining when you get hungry later.”

Zhou Huai pursed his lips and was just about to hold up one finger again when a man came huffing and puffing over from nearby.

He hurried up to the young stall owner, thanking him repeatedly.

“Thanks, kid. Really, thanks for watching the stall for me. There've been so many tourists in this alley lately. You have to queue just to use the bathroom!”

The “deaf” handsome guy spoke quietly.

“It's nothing.”

Fuck.

He could talk?

And his voice was ridiculously nice, too!

“The owner's back.” He left it at that and turned to go. “You can buy from him.”

...

Zhou Huai had never been this embarrassed in his entire life.

He was still standing there in a daze when Qin Yiyu's voice suddenly turned a little more serious in his ear, tinged with irritation.

[That fat bastard's causing trouble again. Don't come back to the shop just yet.]

[Save me a bite of sweet potato.]

After hanging up, Qin Yiyu rubbed his temples.

Then, wearing a bright, shameless grin, he leaned over the counter and waved at the local thug.

“Morning!”

“Back to pay us another visit? Huai's not here right now. Why don't you come back another day?”

“Like hell I'm here to visit!”

The fat man yanked at his collar and immediately launched into a string of curses. After all that shouting, it came down to the same old story: the tattoo was messed up, he wanted compensation, and if he didn't get paid, he wasn't letting it go.

How many times was this now, just this month?

The shop was barely making any money as it was. At this rate, they'd earn less than the amount this guy was extorting from them.

Qin Yiyu gave him a smile.

“Where's the problem? Let me have a look.”

“Right here! Take a good look yourself!”

He actually pulled his clothes open?

Qin Yiyu decided his eyes might get hurt, so he narrowed them.

“Well, how do I put this...”

Leaning against the counter, Qin Yiyu smiled pleasantly.

“A drawing on paper isn't going to look exactly the same when it's drawn on a slab of pork, is it? It's close enough. Cut me some slack, will you?”

The fat man exploded.

“Are you fucking sick in the head?”

Qin Yiyu immediately went along with it.

“Absolutely! You understand me so well! And you figured that out just by looking? A kindred spirit!”

He grabbed the fat man's hand and shook it enthusiastically.

Furious, the man yanked his hand away, snatched up a bottle of ink from beside the counter, and swung it straight at Qin Yiyu's head.

“Fuck your mother!”

What a temper.

Qin Yiyu couldn't be bothered to dodge. He didn't even raise his eyelids.

He'd already prepared himself to take the first hit, collapse onto the floor, and play dead — maybe even accuse the guy of assault and squeeze some compensation out of him.

People who knew how to fight understood better than anyone where to hit someone without killing them.

But the glass bottle didn't come crashing down as expected.

Had he drunk so much last night that even his sense of pain was malfunctioning?

“Who the fuck are—”

Hm?

Qin Yiyu finally lifted his eyelids.

The fat man's greasy hand was frozen in midair, firmly caught by a pale hand with prominent, defined bones.

Before the fat man could finish speaking, he was suddenly shoved aside. He stumbled backward several steps, like a wall on the verge of collapsing, and toppled over before he could regain his balance.

Then another kick came flying at him.

“Fuck!”

At first glance, the kick hadn't even looked particularly forceful. Yet the fat man went straight down, his back hitting the ground with a loud bang. His face twisted, the flesh on it bunching up.

His stomach hurt like hell, and his head was ringing. Before he could even get up, the other person strode over, bent down, grabbed him by the collar, and dragged him out of the shop with one hand.

It looked easier than dragging a dog.

Qin Yiyu couldn't help raising an eyebrow.

That was some efficiency. Like a deranged killer straight out of a movie.

If he hadn't recognized the person immediately, he might have been startled himself.

The fat man sat sprawled on the ground, seeing stars. His neck had turned red from the way his collar had been pulled. It took him several seconds to recover.

The moment he did, he started cursing.

“Who the fuck do you think you are? I'll fucking—”

He hadn't even finished his threat when he saw the other person lift his foot again.

The fat man immediately backed down. Instinctively, he raised a hand to shield himself, and at last, he stopped shouting.

The kick never came.

The foot simply returned to the ground.

The local thug, having failed to get his money, used the doorframe to haul himself upright. He glanced over his shoulder, still hoping to get in a few more curses at Qin Yiyu by shouting around this intimidating stranger.

After all, that bastard was standing there, smiling and waving at him like an idiot.

But the next second, the person in front of him tilted his head, blocking his view.

They were very close.

For the first time, the fat man looked directly into his eyes.

Beneath the brim of his cap, a silver ball piercing sat on his left brow, two metal studs arranged one above the other, gleaming sharply.

Those eyes made the fat man shudder.

They were light brown and narrow, with a faint grey tint around the centre of the irises. They looked almost like the eyes of some wild animal.

“I come here every day,” he said expressionlessly, his voice soft. “See you tomorrow?”

Taken out of context, it sounded almost like a gentle invitation.

Zhou Huai came huffing and puffing back to the shop, just in time to see the fat man limping out of the alley.

He kept glancing over his shoulder, trembling, too distracted to even notice Zhou Huai.

Zhou Huai had thought that was strange enough.

Then he stepped inside and found something even stranger.

“Hey? Isn't that the deaf handsome guy who was helping sell roasted sweet potatoes earlier?”

Qin Yiyu was giving a thumbs-up. At that, he grinned.

“So it was you all along. Wait, why is it always you?”

“Huh?”

Something about that remark sounded off. Zhou Huai mulled it over.

“You two... know each other?”

“This is the guy I told you about. The one who showed up at my place and practically hunted me down.”

Qin Yiyu was still standing by the door, a smile tugging at the corner of his mouth as he looked at the newcomer.

“Nan Yi, right?”

It was a name that was hard to forget.

Zhou Huai's eyes widened. He silently mouthed, “That girl?”

“Girl your ass.”

Qin Yiyu picked up the tissue box beside him and threw it at Zhou Huai.

Nan Yi ignored the whole exchange. He simply answered the last thing he'd said to him, his tone as calm as ever, as though the person who'd just beaten someone up hadn't been him.

“I came to see you. Helping out was just on the way.”

Qin Yiyu had no intention of being so moved by a random act of heroism that he'd offer himself in return.

He stretched.

“Appreciate you standing up for someone in need, but I can't help you with what you're asking. I already made that very clear last time.”

Just thinking about their last encounter gave him a headache.

It had been a long time since he'd met someone this difficult to deal with.

Every time Nan Yi showed up, it was completely unexpected. Last week, he'd practically given him a heart attack.

That day, Qin Yiyu hadn't even woken up properly. He'd forced himself to answer the door, only to find this guy standing outside, dressed in black and wearing a cap, just like today.

The hallway had been pitch-black. He couldn't make out the other man's features. The only thing he could see clearly was the bottle of alcohol in his hand, catching a little light.

Qin Yiyu's first thought had been:

“Come to collect a debt?”

“No.”

The other man tossed the bottle back where it had been — into the cardboard box outside the door that served as a rubbish bin — and dusted off his hands.

Qin Yiyu let out a breath of relief and patted his chest.

“Then why the hell do you look like that? You're creepy.”

He hadn't been able to see the upper half of the man's face, but Nan Yi's answer had stuck with him.

“Born this way.”

Nan Yi didn't look directly at Qin Yiyu. His gaze lingered on the tattoo at Qin Yiyu's throat, then moved down to the one on his wrist.

Then, without any warning, he introduced himself.

“My name is Nan Yi.”

Qin Yiyu hadn't been fully awake that day. Nan Yi had stood in front of him like a robot, rattling off one thing after another, but Qin Yiyu hadn't taken much of it in.

He'd only remembered the name.

And the request that followed: he wanted to form a band with him.

Form a band?

That was somehow even worse than coming to collect a debt.

Qin Yiyu had laughed as though he'd just heard the funniest joke in the world.

“I feel sick just hearing the word 'band'. Can we not? I'll throw up on your shoes in a minute.”

The guy had dug all the way to his doorstep just to drag him out of retirement. He was seriously crazy.

Of course, a few years ago, Qin Yiyu had had no shortage of obsessive fans.

There were people who camped out in the parking lot of his apartment building in the middle of the night, people who pounded on his hotel room door, and people who'd run backstage, stripped off their clothes, and thrown themselves at him.

The list of outrageous things they'd done went on and on.

Later, even after he'd been kicked out of the band, plenty of record labels and producers had gone to great lengths to sign him. They'd tried everything from threats to bribes. There was practically nowhere he could hide.

And then there were the crazies who'd turned from fans into haters because of one rumour or another. Some of them followed him around, getting right up in his personal space. Others threw CDs from his old band straight at his face.

He'd never known a record could draw blood if you threw it hard enough.

Back then, he'd wiped the blood from his forehead and couldn't help marvelling.

“Fuck. These things are really well made.”

Just hearing the word “band” was enough to bring all those unpleasant memories rushing back, amplified by the alcohol.

So much time had passed, yet he still felt like someone who'd died without quite managing to stay dead.

He was stuck halfway through the process of crossing the [[Naihe Bridge|In Chinese mythology, the Naihe Bridge is crossed by souls on their way to reincarnation. Before moving on, they drink Meng Po's soup and forget their past lives.]] and drinking [[Meng Po's soup|Meng Po's Forgetting Soup is a mythical drink that makes the dead forget their memories before reincarnation. Qin Yiyu compares himself to someone who can't quite get through the process of forgetting his past.]].

All he wanted was to forget every last one of those miserable memories. He would've drunk as much soup as they gave him.

But he'd gulped it down too quickly and choked himself back to life.

Qin Yiyu nearly threw up for real.

Whenever he thought about bands, he should have remembered the buzz of an electric guitar.

Instead, all he could hear in his head was the wailing of an ambulance.

So he'd simply said, “Stop showing up at my place. If you come again, I'll call the police.”

Strangely enough, given his expectations, he'd thought Nan Yi would keep pestering him for days.

Every time he opened his front door, he'd braced himself for another encounter.

But the guy really hadn't come back.

A few days passed, and Qin Yiyu had begun to think he'd finally accepted the answer.

Who could've guessed he'd track him down here instead?

How had he even found Zhou Huai's shop?

Qin Yiyu was genuinely curious. This guy might as well become a spy. What was he doing trying to start a band?

“Could you come and take a look at our—”

“Rehearsal” hadn't even left Nan Yi's mouth before Qin Yiyu cut him off without hesitation.

“No.”

“Why?”

“Having to ask why about everything sounds exhausting. Must be hard just being alive.”

Qin Yiyu didn't even raise his eyes.

“If that's the only reason you've come here, then I can tell you this: no matter how many times you show up, my answer will always be the same. I'm not doing it.”

The two faced each other in silence.

As someone who'd been friends with Qin Yiyu for years, Zhou Huai understood him.

After everything he'd been through, Qin Yiyu was no longer the person he'd once been.

Maybe he could be persuaded when it came to other things.

But getting him back into a band?

That was never going to happen. Not in this lifetime.

The summer heat hadn't quite faded. A warm breeze blew a leaf into the shop, sending it spinning across the floor until it landed at Nan Yi's feet.

He lowered his eyes and glanced at it.

“What about something else?”

“Something else? You're getting quite demanding.”

The careless smile remained on Qin Yiyu's face. He was used to talking nonsense.

“Don't tell me you want me to give you a tattoo? See that guy from earlier? He's one of my loyal customers. Wouldn't want you coming back to crack my skull open like he does. That'd be heartbreaking.”

Zhou Huai couldn't stand anyone insulting his work.

“Hey, you little—”

“Those weren't done by you.”

Nan Yi spoke first, his tone certain.

Qin Yiyu frowned.

“How do you know?”

You draw worse than a kindergartener. How could you possibly tattoo someone?

Nan Yi didn't answer.

He turned his head slightly, his gaze passing over the rows of earrings hanging on the wall.

“Pierce my ears.”

Qin Yiyu hadn't expected such a simple request.

“Sure. As long as you pay. Want them done now?”

“No.”

“Then when?”

“Soon.”

With that, Nan Yi turned to leave.

What was with all the cryptic answers?

“Hey. Don't come back here again. I won't be around here much longer, either.”

But Nan Yi said nothing. He didn't even look back.

Qin Yiyu watched him disappear, lost in thought for a long time.

For a brief moment, something about the guy had felt familiar, as though they'd met somewhere before.

But no matter how hard he tried, he couldn't remember.

He even had the bizarre urge to take off Nan Yi's cap and get a proper, thorough look at what he actually looked like.

So he'd know how to avoid him more effectively.

Qin Yiyu tried to give this inexplicable curiosity a reasonable explanation.

Of course, he didn't get the chance.

Nan Yi had already left, quick and decisive as ever.

After all that commotion, the shop was a complete mess. Zhou Huai sighed.

“Tell me you're not a [[walking disaster|丧门星 is a Chinese insult for someone believed to bring bad luck wherever they go. Zhou Huai is joking that Nan Yi attracts trouble just by showing up.]]. You've only been hanging around my shop for a few days, and already you've attracted every kind of weirdo under the sun. Hey, weren't you teaching kids nursery rhymes before? Hurry up and get back to it. My little temple isn't big enough to accommodate a god like you.”

“It's a music theory class, you idiot.”

Qin Yiyu put away the bottle of ink.

“Didn't I tell you? The owner went back to his hometown a couple of days ago, so I didn't have any classes scheduled. He'll be back the day after tomorrow.”

“Right.”

For no particular reason, Zhou Huai suddenly thought of the letter.

He hesitated before speaking.

“By the way... have those debt collectors come looking for you again these past few days?”

“No. I only moved a few days ago. They probably haven't tracked me down yet.”

Qin Yiyu answered absently, then thought of that guy from earlier again.

Maybe Nan Yi didn't need to become a spy after all. If he worked for a debt collection agency, he'd be a champion.

“Oh.”

Zhou Huai swallowed the rest of what he wanted to say, his neck stiff.

Qin Yiyu noticed something was off.

“What?”

“Nothing. Just asking.”

He bent down to sweep up the broken glass. Unexpectedly, he found a black card holder tucked away in the corner.

He opened it, took a look inside, and tossed it straight into Qin Yiyu's arms.

“How does that handsome kid manage to lose things so carelessly?”

Qin Yiyu caught it casually.

The card holder looked old. It was black, with two white embroidered letters, NY, in the bottom-right corner.

Custom-made, too. Probably a gift from some girl.

He unzipped it.

Inside were several bank cards and a folded admission ticket for an English placement test.

The name on the candidate line was Nan Yi.

And the university listed beneath it was Qin Yiyu's own — the university he knew better than any other.

The exam was scheduled for tomorrow morning.

Zhou Huai rested both hands on top of the broom handle and grinned.

“Well, look at that. You're university mates.”

Qin Yiyu couldn't be bothered to respond.

He slipped the admission ticket back inside, zipped up the card holder, tossed it onto the counter, and returned to his reclining chair for another nap.

“Looks pretty important. Maybe he'll come back for it soon.”

With his eyes half closed, Qin Yiyu thought: Who knew?

He'd said he'd come every day.

He'd even said, “See you tomorrow.”

But Qin Yiyu had also told him not to come back.

And the guy seemed like someone who kept his word.

Qin Yiyu habitually squeezed his left hand, rolled over, and decided he couldn't be bothered thinking about it anymore.

Back at the university, Nan Yi parked his car and took his ID card and student card out of his pocket, intending to put them in the inner compartment of his backpack.

When he turned around, he ran straight into Chi Zhiyang.

His newly dyed white hair was impossible to miss.

Chi Zhiyang was sitting on the edge of a flower bed, looking as though he'd been waiting for ages. The moment their eyes met, he sprang to his feet and hurried over.

His hair bounced wildly as he ran, the little braid at the back swinging from side to side like a tail.

He knew Nan Yi had gone to see Qin Yiyu, so he'd skipped class and rushed over.

After all, there wasn't much time left. They were practically at the point of no return.

When Nan Yi didn't say anything, Chi Zhiyang grew anxious.

“Did it work? What did he say? Does he still remember you?”

Three questions at once.

Nan Yi wasn't sure which one to answer. Then again, the answers were more or less the same.

“No.”

It hadn't worked.

He hadn't said anything.

And why would he remember him?

Nan Yi had never intended for Qin Yiyu to remember him in the first place.

There was no time to dwell on the disappointment.

Chi Zhiyang noticed him slipping his ID card into his backpack and frowned.

“Hey, where's your card holder?”

He usually carried it with him everywhere.

Nan Yi put on his backpack.

“Lost it.”

“Lost it?!”

That card holder had been handmade by his grandmother!

As his childhood friend, Chi Zhiyang was even more anxious than Nan Yi himself.

“What are we supposed to do now? Do you remember where you lost it? Can we still find it?”

“Mm.”

Nan Yi's tone was always so certain.

“It'll be returned.”
